Add a per-turn tool-result budget, calibrated by measurement

History had a sliding window and a token budget; the turn itself did not, and
MCP tools answer to nobody — ten calls returning 50k characters each put half a
million characters into one request.

The loop now trims before assembling each request: over
JOY_TOOL_RESULT_TOTAL_CHARS, the largest results become stubs — full text
spilled, head/tail kept in complete lines, one honest sentence saying what was
omitted and where the rest is. Defaults are deliberately large (200k / 30k) so
only pathological output is affected; 0 disables.

Rules taken from the implementations that got there first: only results above
the per-result threshold are touched (deepseek-harness: a hundred medium results
cost more in files than the context saves, so that case is logged and left
alone); stop as soon as the batch fits (their "if total <= max: break"); skip
tools that budget themselves; be idempotent, since this runs every turn; never
cut a line in half (pi's rule — half a JSON object reads worse than one line
fewer); and a failed spill still stubs without promising a path it does not
have.

One real trap, caught by the eval: the stub's target size must be far below the
threshold, or a small configured threshold stubs nothing ("eligible but it
would not fit"). The same eval also caught me asserting that the tool's content
was absent from the prompt — it is present in the tool_use arguments, which is
the assistant's own message, not the result.

Second half: the estimator is now calibrated by measurement. usage.input_tokens
was recorded and never fed back; the loop returns the estimate and the
measurement for the same request, the app-server pairs them in session_context
(migration 0007), and the next turn's budget is corrected by the ratio, clamped
to 0.5-2.0 so one odd request cannot drag it off.

ToolCtx gains session_id (used by the budget log, needed by hooks and jobs
next). 15 new tests plus one eval; docs: configuration, limitations, internals
5 and 9, .env.example, CHANGELOG 0.7.0 — EN + zh.
